※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=14 diag=none
※ pl4g-test: args --conditions=observe

※ An observing check calls a helper that comes *back*, unlike the stopping one,
※ so the call destroys what the convention lets it destroy and the register
※ allocator has to keep nothing live across it.
※
※ `b` is live across the check and is read twice after it, so a build that got
※ the clobber list wrong would answer something other than fourteen.  That is
※ what this test is for, and it is why it runs on all three targets.

let zero: u8 = 0u8
let seven: u8 = 7u8

@[inline(never)]
fn twice(a: u8, b: u8) → u8 pre(a > 0u8):
    a ⊞ b ⊞ b

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(twice(zero, seven), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
